1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,569
Hi, and welcome.

2
00:00:01,569 --> 00:00:07,362
In the next few minutes you'll watch Azure SRE Agent operate a real application on Azure.

3
00:00:07,362 --> 00:00:18,456
It will detect two different production incidents, find the root cause of each, and respond the right way: opening a GitHub issue for a code defect, and repairing Azure configuration for a platform fault.

4
00:00:18,456 --> 00:00:23,060
Everything you'll see was deployed and recorded live from this repository.

5
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:32,262
Azure SRE Agent is an AI site reliability engineer for Azure, generally available since March 2026.

6
00:00:32,262 --> 00:00:43,392
It picks up incidents from Azure Monitor, PagerDuty or ServiceNow, reads your telemetry, your resources and your code, and then acts, but only within the permissions you grant it.

7
00:00:43,392 --> 00:00:53,620
You choose review mode or autonomous mode, every action is audited, and you extend it with skills, subagents, MCP connectors and scheduled tasks.

8
00:00:55,127 --> 00:00:59,199
Our test subject is Contoso Trek, an outdoor gear store.

9
00:00:59,199 --> 00:01:07,877
A Vue front end and a Python FastAPI back end run on Azure Container Apps, and send OpenTelemetry to Application Insights.

10
00:01:07,877 --> 00:01:15,930
Two log alerts watch for HTTP 500 and HTTP 503 errors, and route to the SRE Agent.

11
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:25,942
The agent has its own managed identity with least-privilege roles, read access to the GitHub repository, and a GitHub MCP connector to file issues.

12
00:01:27,437 --> 00:01:31,185
The whole environment is deployed by a GitHub Actions workflow.

13
00:01:31,185 --> 00:01:41,978
It validates the code, the Bicep and the scripts, deploys the infrastructure and the container images, configures the agent through its data plane API, and finishes with a smoke test.

14
00:01:41,978 --> 00:01:46,040
One push to main, and about six minutes later, everything is live.

15
00:01:48,770 --> 00:02:00,378
The agent's behaviour lives in Git as well: global instructions, a knowledge base, two skills, two specialised subagents, and response plans that route each alert to the right subagent.

16
00:02:00,378 --> 00:02:01,953
Notice the tool grants.

17
00:02:01,953 --> 00:02:05,246
The code investigator has no Azure write tools.

18
00:02:05,246 --> 00:02:11,808
The platform operator can run Azure CLI writes, but has no terminal and no GitHub access.

19
00:02:11,808 --> 00:02:16,929
The script reads everything back to prove that what's deployed matches the source.

20
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,036
Here is the storefront.

21
00:02:20,036 --> 00:02:25,120
The banner at the top reflects live health: green means all systems operational.

22
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:30,012
Search, sorting, filtering and product details all work as expected.

23
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:43,014
Scenario one: a code defect.

24
00:02:43,014 --> 00:02:47,136
Some product pages fail with an HTTP 500.

25
00:02:47,136 --> 00:02:56,511
No Azure setting can fix this, so the right response is to find the faulty line of code and open a GitHub issue, without touching any Azure resource.

26
00:02:58,013 --> 00:02:59,705
Let's open a climbing harness.

27
00:02:59,705 --> 00:03:03,203
The product fails to load, and the banner turns amber.

28
00:03:03,203 --> 00:03:09,369
The exception lands in Application Insights, and a few minutes later the Azure Monitor alert fires.

29
00:03:19,147 --> 00:03:23,484
This is the agent's own investigation thread, retrieved from its API.

30
00:03:23,484 --> 00:03:27,450
The alert arrives, and the code investigator subagent takes over.

31
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:31,116
It loads its diagnostic skill and searches the knowledge base.

32
00:03:31,116 --> 00:03:39,553
It queries Log Analytics for the blast radius and the stack trace, then reads the source code: main dot py and catalog dot py.

33
00:03:39,553 --> 00:03:44,241
It searches GitHub for an existing issue, finds none, and files a new one.

34
00:03:44,241 --> 00:03:49,205
Then it hands the fix to GitHub Copilot coding agent, and closes the alert.

35
00:03:49,205 --> 00:03:57,414
From alert to issue and pull request: about two and a half minutes, with no human involved, and no Azure resource modified.

36
00:03:59,713 --> 00:04:02,018
Here is the issue the agent opened.

37
00:04:02,018 --> 00:04:13,701
It has the impact window, the number of failed requests, the root cause with a permalink to catalog dot py, line one hundred nineteen, the stack trace excerpt, and a suggested fix.

38
00:04:13,701 --> 00:04:16,718
It is labelled, and assigned to Copilot.

39
00:04:21,247 --> 00:04:29,021
And GitHub Copilot coding agent has already opened a draft pull request: a guarded division, plus regression tests.

40
00:04:29,021 --> 00:04:31,221
A human reviews and merges it.

41
00:04:31,221 --> 00:04:37,326
The SRE agent diagnoses; Copilot writes the code; your team stays in control.

42
00:04:38,827 --> 00:04:42,671
Scenario two is the opposite case: a platform fault.

43
00:04:42,671 --> 00:04:49,053
A bad configuration change takes the catalog down with HTTP 503 errors.

44
00:04:49,053 --> 00:04:53,632
The code is fine, so the right response is to repair the Azure configuration.

45
00:04:55,127 --> 00:05:03,851
We simulate a risky change: one environment variable on the API container app now points to an inventory backend that this build doesn't support.

46
00:05:03,851 --> 00:05:06,927
Container Apps rolls out a new revision.

47
00:05:08,427 --> 00:05:11,141
Within seconds, the banner turns red.

48
00:05:11,141 --> 00:05:17,631
The catalog is empty, and every catalog request returns HTTP 503.

49
00:05:17,631 --> 00:05:20,215
This is a full outage for customers.

50
00:05:21,707 --> 00:05:27,810
The availability alert fires, and this time the response plan routes it to the platform operator subagent.

51
00:05:27,810 --> 00:05:38,868
It confirms the 503 errors and the configuration error in the logs, checks the Activity Log to find the change that caused it, and restores the correct value with an Azure CLI update.

52
00:05:38,868 --> 00:05:43,844
Then it verifies that the new revision is healthy and the catalog is serving again.

53
00:05:46,387 --> 00:05:48,632
And the storefront is back to green.

54
00:05:48,632 --> 00:05:53,740
No redeploy, no code change, and no engineer paged in the middle of the night.

55
00:05:58,667 --> 00:06:00,984
What makes this safe is governance.

56
00:06:00,984 --> 00:06:06,405
The agent uses a managed identity with built-in roles, scoped to one resource group.

57
00:06:06,405 --> 00:06:09,314
Each subagent only has the tools it needs.

58
00:06:09,314 --> 00:06:16,117
Start in review mode, where a human approves each action, and move to autonomous mode where you're confident.

59
00:06:16,117 --> 00:06:20,107
And every query and command is visible in the thread for audit.

60
00:06:21,633 --> 00:06:24,239
Pricing is based on Azure Agent Units.

61
00:06:24,239 --> 00:06:34,708
Each agent consumes four units per hour while it exists, which is about two thousand nine hundred units a month, or roughly two hundred ninety dollars at ten cents per unit in East US.

62
00:06:34,708 --> 00:06:43,746
On top of that, active work is metered by tokens: an incident investigation costs roughly twelve to thirty-five units depending on the model.

63
00:06:43,746 --> 00:06:48,866
You can cap monthly usage, and a thirty-day trial waives the always-on charge.

64
00:06:48,866 --> 00:06:51,967
Always confirm regional prices in the calculator.

65
00:06:53,467 --> 00:06:54,931
Plan for a few limits.

66
00:06:54,931 --> 00:06:58,669
The agent is available in more than twenty Azure regions.

67
00:06:58,669 --> 00:07:02,515
It can only act where its identity has permissions, which is the point.

68
00:07:02,515 --> 00:07:08,741
Alert-driven flows inherit your alert latency, typically five to ten minutes for log alerts.

69
00:07:08,741 --> 00:07:12,635
Connectors have tool budgets, and some newer features are still in preview.

70
00:07:14,137 --> 00:07:21,911
To get started, fork this repository and run the deploy workflow, or point the agent at one of your own resource groups in review mode.

71
00:07:21,911 --> 00:07:26,623
Encode your runbooks as skills, and route your alerts with response plans.

72
00:07:26,623 --> 00:07:27,716
Thanks for watching.
